export const DE_C2_S06_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-achtundachtzig": [
    `On the Hallig of Hooge there are ten dwelling mounds, a church, a school with three pupils and, since 1978, an aerial that is taller than the church tower. It belongs to Jens Ketelsen, and it is the reason why the people of Hooge, when they come back from the mainland and see the Hallig rising out of the Wadden Sea as flat as a pancake, look first for the mast and then say: well, Jens is still there. Jens was always there. He had not been to the mainland since 1983, except twice to the dentist in Husum, and both times he had regretted it.`,
    `Every evening at nine he sat down at the radio and sent his call sign with the Morse key, DL3JK, and every evening at nine and a few seconds the answer came from Lustenau in Vorarlberg, a good nine hundred kilometres away, at the other end of the country and then a bit further across the border: OE9LMH. Her name was Liesl; at first he knew no more than that, and she did not want to say any more. She only transmitted in telegraphy. A microphone, she had morsed to him in the first week, she did not own and did not want to own; the voice gave away too much, the key only what was necessary. Jens, who did not like talking anyway, thought that was the most sensible sentence a woman had ever said to him.`,
    `It went on like that for forty-six years. They morsed about the weather, which in Lustenau consisted of the föhn and on Hooge of wind, about the storm floods that washed the water up to his front door, about the embroidery works where she worked, about her husband, who died in 1994, and about his wife, whom he had never had. They argued about football and about the Pope. They had never seen each other, never exchanged a photo, never written a letter. Every evening they said goodbye with 73, which among radio amateurs means "best regards", and once, on New Year's Eve 1999, after a bottle of grog, Jens had sent 88, which means "love and kisses", and Liesl had been silent for a minute and then sent 73, and he had never tried again.`,
    `On 4 March 2025, OE9LMH did not answer. Nor on the 5th, nor on the 6th. Every evening Jens called for an hour on the usual frequency and then on all the others, and heard only the hiss of the band, which sounds as if the universe were breathing through its teeth. On 12 March he packed a bag, went to the jetty and took the ferry to Schlüttsiel. It was the first time in forty-two years that he had left the Hallig without toothache. It took him fourteen hours and four trains, and when he arrived in Lustenau the land seemed so steep to him and the mountains so pushy, as if they had placed themselves there especially for him.`,
    `He knew the address from the call-sign directory. A neighbour who was watering the tulips in Liesl's front garden looked him up and down for a while and then said, in a dialect of which he understood at most half, that Frau Hämmerle was in the care home in Dornbirn, since the stroke. She couldn't speak any more, or write either; the right hand was completely gone and the left "just twitches". Her son had taken down all the radio stuff; he lived in Vienna and had no time for that sort of thing.`,
    `At the care home a nurse led him into a room looking out onto a rock face. Liesl Hämmerle was white-haired and smaller than he had imagined her, and he had, as he now realised, not imagined her at all for forty-six years, only heard her. She looked at him without recognising him; how could she have? Her left hand lay on the blanket, and the fingers were tapping, incessantly, short and long, short and long. "She does that all day," said the nurse. "The doctor says it's neurological."`,
    `Jens listened. It did not take him long. Long-short-long-short, long-long-short-long: CQ, CQ, this is OE9LMH. She was calling. She had been calling for three weeks, every day, on a blanket in Dornbirn, and nobody had answered. Jens sat down on the chair beside the bed, put his index finger on the plastic bedside table and tapped, slowly and clearly, as he had done for forty-six years: OE9LMH, this is DL3JK. The hand on the blanket stopped. Then, after a pause he knew, it tapped back, trembling but without a mistake: JENS?`,
    `The nurse said later that she had never seen anything like it: two old people tapping on furniture for an hour and laughing as they did so, the man quite silently and the woman without a sound. At the end of the visit, when Jens already had his coat on, Liesl tapped once more, just two digits, the same one twice. Jens stood in the doorway for a while. Then he went to the station and asked about a room nearby, for a longer stay, and on Hooge people looked for the mast and were surprised that it was still standing although Jens was no longer there.`,
  ],
  "de-der-preis-eines-kaisers": [
    `I was nineteen when I helped to buy an emperor, and I did not call it that at the time. In the counting house of Herr Jakob Fugger on the Weinmarkt in Augsburg it was not called a purchase but "the Frankfurt transaction", and whoever called it anything else did not do so for long. I was an under-clerk, which means I copied out what others had calculated and checked the sums of what others had copied, and for this I was paid sixteen guilders a year, board and a room under the roof in which one could not sleep in summer or write in winter.`,
    `In June of the year 1519, Emperor Maximilian had been dead for half a year, and the seven prince-electors were to elect a new one in Frankfurt. There were two candidates, the young King Charles of Spain and the King of France, and both had grasped that the electors' votes were not to be won by prayers. The Frenchman paid in crowns, which he had, and in promises, which he did not have. Charles paid with bills of exchange from the house of Fugger, and in those days a Fugger bill counted for more between Antwerp and Venice than the word of a king, because, unlike the word of a king, it was honoured.`,
    `My task in those weeks was to keep the accounts. Mainz got so much, Cologne so much, the Count Palatine so much and, because he played hard to get, the same again; on top of that the electors' councillors, the councillors' councillors, the messengers, the innkeepers with whom the messengers lodged, and a canon in Trier whose role I never understood but who was very assertive. In the end my book showed a little over five hundred thousand guilders, and on 28 June Charles was elected unanimously in Frankfurt, which Herr Fugger acknowledged with a nod, the way one acknowledges the arrival of a cargo of pepper.`,
    `But when I added up the total, three guilders and twelve kreuzers were missing. Three guilders out of five hundred thousand is, as old Lenz the bookkeeper explained to me with a shrug, less than a flea on an ox. You write it under expenses and go to sleep. I did not sleep. I had learned from Herr Fugger, or believed I had, that a book that does not balance is not slightly wrong but wholly wrong, just as a glass with a crack is not slightly broken. I went through the entries for three nights by a candle that I paid for out of my own wages, and found nothing.`,
    `On the fourth night I found it. It was not in the Frankfurt book but in the petty cash of the counting house, from which Herr Fugger, and only he, occasionally took money without writing it down, because, as Lenz said, he was more precise in his head anyway than all of us on paper. On the Tuesday before the election he had taken three guilders and twelve kreuzers. I knew what for, because I had seen him from the window: he had gone across to the Jakobervorstadt, where his people were building the new settlement that was later called the Fuggerei, with houses in which poor citizens of Augsburg were to live for one guilder a year and in return say three prayers daily for the soul of the founder. There he had given the money to a widow whose husband had fallen from the scaffolding.`,
    `In the morning I went to him with the book. As always, he had been sitting at his desk since five o'clock, in the golden cap he never took off, and he let me explain the matter without interrupting me. I said I did not know under which entry to book the sum. It did not belong under the Frankfurt transaction, nor under expenses, and there was no heading for alms in this book, because it was a book about prince-electors.`,
    `Herr Fugger looked at me for a long time. Then he took the pen, dipped it and wrote below the last line, below Mainz and Cologne and the Count Palatine and the canon of Trier, in his small, hard handwriting: "Item, three guilders twelve kreuzers, for the soul. Is also an election." Then he pushed the book back to me. It was the only time in seven years that I saw him smile, and I have thought for a long time about whether it was a smile or a calculation.`,
    `I have grown old in the service of the house. I have kept the accounts for mines in Tyrol and copper from Hungary, cardinals' hats and indulgence money, and over time I have learned to bring everything under one entry. The emperor we bought died long ago, and of the five hundred thousand guilders the house did not, as is well known, see them all again. But the widow lived in the Fuggerei until her death, for one guilder a year, and she said her three prayers every day for a man who, as she once told me, never really looked at her. I do not know which of the two investments was the better one. The book, and that is the most important thing, balanced.`,
  ],
  "de-das-letzte-eis": [
    `The ice lay in a glass case in the basement of the national park centre in Heiligenblut, at minus eighteen degrees, and it was, as the sign said, the last piece of the Pasterze, once Austria's largest glacier. It had been drilled out in 2031 from a depth of forty metres, when there was still a depth of forty metres to drill: a cylinder as long as a child's arm and as thick as a thigh, cloudy, bluish, with tiny bubbles in it, as if someone had frozen mineral water. In the summer of 2140 a few hundred people came every day to look at it. There was nothing more of the Pasterze to see. Up where it had been lay scree, grey as far as the eye could see, and a sign that read: Glacier level 1856.`,
    `Theresia Pichler had looked after the ice for nineteen years. She was a glaciologist, a profession with about as much future as that of a coachman around 1920, and she knew it and did not make jokes about it, because everyone else already made them. Her grandfather had still skied on the Pasterze. Her father had watched it fall apart. She herself knew it only from the case, and sometimes, when the museum had closed, she put her hand against the glass and felt the cold, the way one lays a hand on the stone of a grave.`,
    `On 9 August, a Tuesday, the thunderstorm came. It was not a storm of the kind Theresia's grandfather had known, but one of the new kind that did not pass over but settled in. At 3 p.m. the power went off. At 3.04 the emergency generator started up, at 3.40 it went off again with a sound like a sigh, and Ferdl Gruber, the caretaker, lay under it for an hour and emerged with the news that a part had broken that could be obtained in Lienz. The road to Lienz had been buried since 3.20. A mudslide, said the radio. Several days, said the radio.`,
    `In the basement the temperature rose. Theresia calculated: with the door closed, without cooling, in the summer of 2140, the ice had perhaps twelve hours. As long as the network held, she phoned Klagenfurt, Vienna, a laboratory in Innsbruck that was sorry, and one in Zurich that was even sorrier. Then she sat on the floor in the half-dark next to the case and watched drops forming on the glass, on the outside first, as if the glass were sweating with fear.`,
    `Ferdl's granddaughter found her there. Her name was Mira, she was eleven and was spending the holidays with her grandfather because in Heiligenblut, as her mother said, it was at least still below thirty degrees at night. She sat down beside Theresia and looked at the ice. "What are the bubbles?" she asked. Air, said Theresia. Air that had been trapped when the snow fell, about six hundred years ago, and had been stuck in there ever since. Mira thought about this. "So the air from the old days is in there?" she asked. "From the knights and that?" Theresia said yes, roughly, from the knights and that. "And when it melts, does it come out?"`,
    `At two in the morning, when the storm finally died down, they carried the ice up. Theresia had put it in a cool box that no longer cooled, and Ferdl drove them in the old off-road vehicle up the Glockner road to the Franz-Josefs-Höhe. From there they went on foot, with head torches, for half an hour over the scree, to the spot where, according to Theresia's map, the ice had still lain in the year 2000, more than a hundred metres thick. There they laid the cylinder on a flat stone. It gleamed in the light of the torches. It was already dripping.`,
    `They sat down beside it. Nobody said anything. Towards four it grew light, and the ice grew smaller, and the drops ran over the stone, gathered in a groove and disappeared between the rocks, downhill, towards where the river Möll used to have its source. Every now and then there was a faint crackle as a bubble burst. Mira had lain down on her stomach with her face right up close to the ice, and every time it crackled she breathed in with great seriousness. "I'm breathing knight air," she said.`,
    `By half past six the ice was gone. On the stone there remained a dark, damp patch that dried in the morning sun faster than Theresia could watch. In the museum today there is an empty glass case, and next to it hangs a small sign that Mira wrote in felt-tip pen in capital letters and that Theresia, against the wishes of the management, will not let anyone take down: Here was the ice. It has gone home. I've got the air.`,
  ],
  "de-nu-herr-professor": [
    `I had prepared myself for many things when I took up my first post at the Technical University of Dresden in October: for students who took philosophy as a compulsory module alongside mechanical engineering and looked at me accordingly; for seminar rooms that smelled of linoleum and prefab concrete; for the dialect that colleagues in Munich had warned me about with a pitying smile. I was not prepared for Frau Pietzsch.`,
    `She sat in the front row of my seminar "Basic Questions of Ethics", a small woman of eighty-three with a purple quilted jacket, a thermos flask and a lunch box that smelled of liver sausage. She was a guest student. She had, as she informed me unasked in the first session, worked for forty years as a switching supervisor at the state energy combine, brought up three children and outlived two husbands, and now she wanted, and here she said it in a way I had to translate three times in my head, "to finally know what Kant actually wanted".`,
    `It was an instructive semester, above all for me. Frau Pietzsch did not raise her hand; she simply said "Nu", in a tone that, depending on the context, could mean agreement, doubt or a devastating critique of practical reason. When I wrote the categorical imperative on the board, she said "Nu", and it was agreement. When I explained it with an example about promises, she said "Nu", and it was doubt. When a student from Chemnitz claimed that morality was ultimately a question of evolution, she said "Nu", and the student did not speak again for the rest of the semester.`,
    `In December we came to Kant's famous essay on the supposed right to lie out of love for humanity. I presented the case as it is found in every textbook: a murderer stands at the door and asks whether my friend, whom he is pursuing, is hiding in my house. May I lie? Kant says no, not even then. The students were outraged, as students have been at this point for two hundred years, and I had my counter-arguments ready, neatly numbered. Then Frau Pietzsch put down her thermos flask.`,
    `"I did that once," she said. "Nineteen seventy-four. Two gentlemen were standing at the door, not with a knife, with ID cards, and wanted to know whether the neighbour, Gerhard, had watched West German TV at our place and what he had said about the Party. I said: Well, Gerhard? He only watches the Sandman and falls asleep." She paused. The engineering students, who usually looked at their phones under the table, looked up. "I lied. Through my teeth."`,
    `I said something cautious about how Kant in such a case might possibly, under certain circumstances, if one took the later Kant into account … She raised her hand. "Wait, Doctor, there's more." After reunification, she said, she had read her file, at the Gauck authority, as it was called then. She had read it very thoroughly, with reading glasses and a liver-sausage sandwich. And on page one hundred and twelve it said that an unofficial collaborator with the codename "Television Set" reported that the citizen Pietzsch had demonstrably given false information to the authorities. The unofficial collaborator, she said, had been Gerhard.`,
    `It was very quiet in the seminar room. I still had my counter-arguments in my hand, neatly numbered, and they suddenly seemed to me like an umbrella in a storm flood. "And now," said Frau Pietzsch, "I want to know from you: did I do the right thing, or Kant?" I said that, to be honest, I did not know. She nodded, satisfied, as if I had finally passed an exam. "Nu," she said, and it sounded like praise. "I don't know either. I just know I'd do it again. And that Kant still wasn't stupid. You have to live with that. That's not in any book."`,
    `In the exam in February there was a single question, and it was not in any textbook. Frau Pietzsch did not take part, because guest students are not allowed to sit exams; she sat in the front row anyway, with her thermos flask, and at half past ten, without asking, handed out liver-sausage sandwiches to everyone who was stuck. I know that this was against the examination regulations. After careful consideration of all the maxims in question, I did not report it.`,
  ],
  "de-suetterlin": [
    `Kurt Ladewig had been at the Stralsund city archive for forty years, and since he had retired, people came to his home with their shoeboxes. They were always the same shoeboxes: letters from the front, cookbooks, autograph albums, diaries of grandmothers who had nothing to hide from their grandchildren because they knew the grandchildren could not read their handwriting. Ladewig could read it. He was one of the last people in Western Pomerania who read Kurrent and Sütterlin as fluently as the newspaper, and he took no money for it, only cake, which, at his age and with his blood sugar, was the more expensive currency, as his daughter thought.`,
    `Jonas Brandt brought no cake but a plastic sleeve, and in it a single sheet. He was in his mid-thirties, a teacher in Greifswald, and he explained the matter with the thoroughness of a man who had often explained it to himself. The villa on the Sound, family property since 1880. The great-great-grandfather, Heinrich Brandt, shipowner, two sons. It was generally assumed that the elder, Jonas's great-grandfather, should have inherited the house. Instead it had gone to the younger, Wilhelm, on the basis of this will, and his descendants still lived in it today. Great-aunt Hedwig, Wilhelm's daughter, ninety-four years old, kept the original like a relic. He had been allowed to borrow it for a family chronicle. Only nobody could read it.`,
    `Ladewig put on his reading glasses. "Stralsund, 3 May 1912. My last will." The handwriting was beautiful, very even, the letters upright and round, the ascenders and descenders in exactly the proportion that had been taught to generations of schoolchildren with a ruler. Ladewig read the will to the end, slowly, and then once more, and then he took off his glasses and cleaned them, although they were clean. Jonas asked whether something was wrong. Ladewig said he needed to look at it in peace and asked him to leave the sheet with him for a week.`,
    `What was wrong was the handwriting itself. The graphic artist Ludwig Sütterlin had designed his script on behalf of the Prussian Ministry of Culture, and it was introduced in Prussian schools in 1915. Before that, people wrote Kurrent, pointed, slanted and narrow, a script like a picket fence in a crosswind. A shipowner who drew up his will in 1912 at the age of fifty-seven had learned to write around 1862. He could no more write in Sütterlin than a person in 1912 could write an email. Whoever had written this sheet had gone to school between 1915 and 1941.`,
    `Ladewig did not call Jonas but the villa. Hedwig Brandt received him in the conservatory, sitting upright in a wicker chair, with a view of the Sound and of Rügen beyond it, and before he could say anything, she said: "You're the man with the old scripts. I thought you would come." She offered him tea, not cake. He put the sheet on the table. She looked at it with a kind of professional appreciation. "I was eighteen," she said. "And I had the most beautiful handwriting in the whole class."`,
    `It had been in 1949. Her father had not come back from the war, her mother was ill, the shipping company expropriated, and the uncle to whom the house should have gone was sitting in Hamburg writing letters in which the word "sale" appeared. The real will had been in the desk of her grandfather, who had died in the spring of 1945 at the age of ninety, at a time when nobody bothered about documents. Hedwig had copied it out, word for word, with ink from the office and on paper from the same drawer, and changed only one name. "And added one line," she said. "That the piano should go to the granddaughter Hedwig. I allowed myself that. After all, I got nothing else out of it except my conscience."`,
    `Ladewig asked where the real will was. Hedwig stood up, with an effort she did not want to show, went to the piano, lifted the lid and pulled out a yellowed envelope. "One does not burn documents," she said. "I'm a Brandt, not a barbarian." She gave him both. He should do with them what an archivist does. Three weeks later she was dead. She died, as people in Stralsund told each other, at the piano, which was not true; she died in hospital, but people found the other version more beautiful, and Ladewig, who knew how tradition comes about, did not contradict them.`,
    `He did what an archivist does. He handed the real will, catalogued and dated, to the city archive as part of the Brandt papers, with a closure period of a hundred years, as is not unusual for documents containing personal data. Jonas Brandt got his sheet back, together with a clean, letter-for-letter transcription into Latin script, as he had requested. He said thank you with a cake. He had, as Ladewig always emphasised later, asked to have read to him what was on the sheet, not to be told when it had been written. And Ladewig was a precise man.`,
  ],
};

